**SAM rule.** Only ONE abstract method. Default methods (have body) do NOT count. Static methods do NOT count. Methods inherited from `Object` (`toString`, `equals`, `hashCode`) do NOT count. If an interface declares an abstract method that matches an Object method, it does NOT count toward the SAM limit.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface TradeValidator {
    boolean validate(Trade trade);        // ONE abstract method — SAM (Recommended) 

    default TradeValidator and(TradeValidator other) {  // default — does NOT count
        return trade -> this.validate(trade) && other.validate(trade);
    }

    static TradeValidator always() { return trade -> true; }  // static — does NOT count

    String toString();  // matches Object.toString() — does NOT count
}
// Lambda: TradeValidator v = trade -> trade.isActive();
```

**@FunctionalInterface — compile-time safety.** Without it: accidentally adding a second abstract method silently breaks lambda compatibility. With it: compile ERROR immediately.

```java
@FunctionalInterface
interface TradeProcessor {
    void process(Trade trade);
    // void audit(Trade trade);  // COMPILE ERROR! @FunctionalInterface with 2 abstract methods!
}
```

**Can extend another interface.** Yes, as long as the result has exactly one abstract method.

```java
interface Identifiable { String getId(); }  // 1 abstract method

@FunctionalInterface
interface TradeAction extends Identifiable {
    void execute();  // + getId() from parent = 2 abstract methods!
}
// COMPILE ERROR! Two abstract methods: execute() + getId()
// FIX: override getId() as default in TradeAction

@FunctionalInterface
interface TradeAction extends Identifiable {
    void execute();
    default String getId() { return "DEFAULT"; }  // now only execute() is abstract (Recommended) 
}
```

**Core interfaces from java.util.function — with finance examples:**

`Predicate<Trade>`: `trade -> trade.isSettled()` — filtering settled trades.

`Function<Trade, BigDecimal>`: `Trade::getAmount` — extracting amount from trade.

`Consumer<Trade>`: `trade -> notificationService.send(trade)` — sending notification.

`Supplier<String>`: `() -> UUID.randomUUID().toString()` — generating trade ID.

`BiFunction<Trade, BigDecimal, Trade>`: `(trade, fee) -> trade.withFee(fee)` — applying fee to trade.

`UnaryOperator<BigDecimal>`: `amount -> amount.multiply(taxRate)` — applying tax to amount.

`BinaryOperator<BigDecimal>`: `BigDecimal::add` — summing two amounts (used in reduce).

**Composition — chaining functional interfaces:**

```java
Predicate<Trade> isActive = Trade::isActive;
Predicate<Trade> isLarge = t -> t.getAmount().compareTo(threshold) > 0;
Predicate<Trade> isActiveLarge = isActive.and(isLarge);      // AND composition
Predicate<Trade> isInactive = isActive.negate();              // NOT
Predicate<Trade> isActiveOrLarge = isActive.or(isLarge);     // OR
Predicate<Trade> notBlank = Predicate.not(String::isBlank);  // static not()

Function<Trade, BigDecimal> getAmount = Trade::getAmount;
Function<Trade, String> amountAsString = getAmount.andThen(BigDecimal::toString);  // chain
```

